        io.github.kotoba-lang/osaho
        {:git/url "https://github.com/kotoba-lang/osaho.git"
         ;; Advanced 2026-09-09, d809f284 -> 1a81e2d (osaho main, forward only,
         ;; 46 commits). The reason is the ORACLE, not a feature here: this
         ;; repo's string-search tests never write an expected value down, they
         ;; run `kotoba.kir/execute` on both the operation and the rewrite and
         ;; compare. `string-index-of` had no evaluator at d809f284, so the
         ;; rows added for it below would have compared two refusals and passed
         ;; while proving nothing -- the exact shape of "a test that could not
         ;; run returns what a test that ran and found nothing returns". osaho
         ;; 375cb65 added the evaluator (first UTF-8 byte offset, -1 absent,
         ;; empty-needle trap) and ee850cb fixed its ClojureScript
         ;; representation to a BigInt i64, which is what makes the oracle
         ;; agree on both hosts.
         ;;
         ;; The tip commit is the one that makes the astral rows below pass.
         ;; `utf8-index-of!` charged a surrogate pair 4 bytes at the high
         ;; surrogate and 3 more at the low one, so it answered 7 where this
         ;; repo's lowering, kotoba-script's JS emitter and CPython all answer
         ;; 4. That disagreement is what the oracle comparison here surfaced,
         ;; and osaho ADR 0271 is the fix. Pinning a37577c instead would leave
         ;; one row red for a defect that is not in this repository.
         :git/sha "1a81e2d7df947a719b75db1e6e5616a900f3ebb7"}
        io.github.kotoba-lang/kotoba-gmir
        {:git/url "https://github.com/kotoba-lang/kotoba-gmir.git"
         :git/sha "a3f0597cc8fdbd104235996441673af94f6db621"}
        ;; kotoba-mir 4bf67c0: call+loop uses the scanner (:call-live), the
        ;; conservative fallback colors frame slots from CFG liveness, and
        ;; pure integer SSA segments are scheduled per basic block after physical
        ;; allocation behind hard effect barriers, with RAW/WAR/WAW physical
        ;; register hazards preserved. NBB guards integer immediates before
        ;; physical-register hash lookup. Released callee-saved registers stay in
        ;; the preserved tier and are reused before adding another frame save.
        ;; On AArch64, entry parameters live across a call start in that
        ;; preserved tier, avoiding a store/reload pair on every loop iteration.
        ;; Unique-use zero equality followed by branch-zero becomes an explicit
        ;; AArch64-only branch-nonzero while SSA identities are still available.
        ;; AArch64 self-tail edges now receive the allocator's explicit
        ;; parameter-home vector and use its cycle-safe parallel-copy schedule;
        ;; backed/incomplete sites retain the public-entry tail path. x86 retains
        ;; its execution-qualified scratch-first selection. A unique-use integer
        ;; producer in one closed self-recur suffix may write its exact parameter
        ;; home directly when the current owner dies at that producer. Counted
        ;; recurrence proof may admit a real direct call only when the closed
        ;; module proves its callee is a pure, nontrapping, call-free leaf.
        ;; This repository still does not execute compiled programs. A green
        ;; native suite here is encodings only, not that measurement.
        ;; 8a2bc4d sends straight-line call functions through the scanner:
        ;; call-crossing values live in preserved registers instead of stable
        ;; slots (+6.66% measured on the qualified call fixture); the
        ;; superseded allocate-call-live is deleted.
        ;; 4a75623 steers quotient-leaf values away from RAX/RDX, arming
        ;; the dead-quotient-save elision below it.
        ;; e266a86: the parallel copy's cycle-breaking temporary is carried
        ;; into the body only when something reserved it. Carried unreserved it
        ;; named the slot the body's first spill was then handed, and the next
        ;; cycle break stored over a live value -- measured on aiueos
        ;; hkdf-sha256.kotoba, whose `hmac-mode` passed the literal 92 as its
        ;; own context pointer (kotoba-mir ADR-0017).
        io.github.kotoba-lang/kotoba-mir
        {:git/url "https://github.com/kotoba-lang/kotoba-mir.git"
         ;;
         ;; Advanced 2026-09-08, 0bb174c8 -> ac690136 (kotoba-mir main, ahead
         ;; 2, forward only), and this one is a CORRECTNESS advance, not a
         ;; feature one. `aarch64-fuse-zero-equality-branches` tested its
         ;; constant with `zero?`, which is false for a JavaScript BigInt, so
         ;; on the JVM-free front the fusion never fired for a literal that
         ;; came from the reader -- every literal in real source. Measured
         ;; through amu: `(if (= a 0) a a)` was 24 bytes here and 12 on the
         ;; JVM, for the same program.
         ;;
         ;; That made emission a function of the HOST'S SPELLING of a value
         ;; rather than of the value, which `kotoba-verifier` -- it re-derives
         ;; the instruction stream from sealed KIR and refuses drift --
         ;; correctly reports as "native instruction stream rejected" the
         ;; moment an artifact is read back with a reader that preserves i64
         ;; exactly. `peephole.cljc`'s own docstring names this hazard for
         ;; iteration order; representation is the same hazard by another
         ;; route.
         ;;
         ;; Advanced 2026-09-09, ac690136 -> 55d78851 (kotoba-mir main, ahead
         ;; 2, forward only). `select-target` no longer refuses
         ;; `:gmir/kernel-dot-f32` for AArch64 -- this repo's
         ;; `a64-kernel-dot-f32` is the arm that earned the lift, and without
         ;; this pin the operation is selected here and rejected there. The
         ;; fused dequantize-and-dot family is still refused, for a reason
         ;; that is now narrower and is stated at the refusal.
         ;;
         ;; Advanced 2026-09-09, 55d78851 -> b70a567a (kotoba-mir main, ahead
         ;; 2, forward only). `select-target` now admits
         ;; `:gmir/kernel-dequant-dot-q8-0` on AArch64 as well, which is what
         ;; `a64-kernel-dequant-dot` here is the arm for. The refusal is
         ;; spelled by subtraction upstream, so the six formats this backend
         ;; still has no arms for are refused BEFORE selection rather than at
         ;; `a64-dequant-formats` -- both refusals exist and are asserted, and
         ;; that is deliberate: one is a target decision and the other is an
         ;; encoder's admission that it has no bytes.
         :git/sha "b70a567a9a2e8ba20e6dbb6257ed0eddd9d0c8d5"}
        io.github.kotoba-lang/kotoba-codegen
